129 #t _kc$130 _kc$131 _kc$132 "Accept forever, handing each connection and its peer address to serve!.\n  accept() fills the sockaddr, which is where :remote-addr comes from \x2014; it\n  used to be passed ffi/null and the Ring field was a hardcoded literal.\n\n  ONE CONNECTION'S FAULT IS NOT THE SERVER'S, and it used to be. A throw out\n  of serve! unwound this loop, the finally freed the sockaddr, and accepting\n  ended for the life of the process \x2014; silently, because the loop runs in a\n  future nothing derefs until stop-server. What that looks like from outside\n  is exactly a server under load: the port is still held, server-stats still\n  answers, in-flight requests still finish, and every connection after it sits\n  in the listen backlog, where it costs the process no fd and shows up in no\n  count. Measured with one injected throw: the next request, and every request\n  after it, timed out, and the only report of it came out of stop-server as an\n  ExecutionException. So serve! runs under a guard, and a loop that ends while\n  the server is running says so." _kc$133 82 _kc$134 1 _kc$135 "ring_chez/adapter.clj")))))) _kc$136))) (jolt-register-source! "serve-loop" "ring-chez.adapter" "serve-loop" "ring_chez/adapter.clj" 82) (jolt-register-callsite! "serve-loop" 59 "sockaddr-ip" #f) (jolt-register-callsite! "serve-loop" 60 "ex-info" #f) (jolt-register-callsite! "serve-loop" 99 "nth" #f) (jolt-register-callsite! "serve-loop" 102 "write" #f) (jolt-register-callsite! "serve-loop" 103 "c-accept" #f) (jolt-register-callsite! "serve-loop" 103 "deref" #f) (jolt-register-callsite! "serve-loop" 103 "nth" #f) (jolt-register-callsite! "serve-loop" 108 "neg?" #f) (jolt-register-callsite! "serve-loop" 109 "poll-one" #f) (jolt-register-callsite! "serve-loop" 111 "neg?" #f) (jolt-register-callsite! "serve-loop" 116 "$Rnot" #f) (jolt-register-callsite! "serve-loop" 116 "c-close" #f) (jolt-register-callsite! "serve-loop" 116 "deref" #f) (jolt-register-callsite! "serve-loo